WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:06.280
Container security protects the full lifecycle: source, build, image, registry, runtime,

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:11.940
host, network, secrets, orchestration, monitoring, and response.

00:00:12.160 --> 00:00:15.120
A container image may look like a single artifact,

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:20.300
but it represents many decisions made by developers, platform teams, security teams,

00:00:20.520 --> 00:00:23.320
operations teams, and service providers.

00:00:23.540 --> 00:00:28.180
Strong programs make those decisions visible, reviewable, and repeatable.

00:00:28.400 --> 00:00:32.800
Trusted images and secure builds give deployments a safer starting point.

00:00:33.020 --> 00:00:38.060
Base image hygiene, dependency management, vulnerability scanning, image provenance,

00:00:38.280 --> 00:00:41.960
signing and verification concepts, registry access control,

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:47.380
and CI/CD protections reduce the chance that untrusted or vulnerable artifacts reach

00:00:47.600 --> 00:00:48.560
production.

00:00:48.780 --> 00:00:50.860
Containers make deployment easy;

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:55.400
security ensures that what gets deployed is the thing the organization intended to run.

00:00:55.620 --> 00:00:57.700
Runtime controls limit impact.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.560
Running as non-root where practical, dropping unnecessary capabilities,

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:07.420
using resource limits, avoiding privileged configurations, protecting host mounts,

00:01:07.640 --> 00:01:09.560
separating secrets from images,

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:14.500
and controlling network communication all reduce what a workload can do if it fails or is

00:01:14.720 --> 00:01:15.840
compromised.

00:01:16.060 --> 00:01:21.100
Orchestration access control and admission policy help make those expectations consistent

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:23.320
across many workloads.

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:28.580
Finally, containers need monitoring and response, not just preventive controls.

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:34.000
Inventory, logs, events, drift detection, vulnerability management,

00:01:34.220 --> 00:01:38.300
and repeatable redeployment help teams operate safely at container speed.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.600
The goal is not to block containers.

00:01:40.820 --> 00:01:45.540
The goal is to make containerized applications minimal, observable, recoverable,

00:01:45.760 --> 00:01:48.400
and aligned with real security risk decisions.
